Yes. “Memory” is really a bundle of different storage and reconstruction systems, and visual memory is only one slice of it.

You can have auditory memory for voices, melodies, rhythms, and timing; olfactory and gustatory memory for smells and tastes; tactile memory for texture, pressure, temperature, and contact; proprioceptive memory for where your body was and how a movement felt; vestibular memory for balance, acceleration, spinning, and orientation; and interoceptive memory for internal states like tension, fatigue, hunger, calm, adrenaline, or a particular bodily “feeling.”

Then there are memories that are not tied to a single sense at all: spatial memory for layouts and routes, procedural memory for how to do things, semantic memory for facts and concepts, episodic memory for events, emotional memory for the affective signature of an experience, and social memory for faces, relationships, reputations, roles, and interaction patterns.

There is also something close to predictive memory: not just remembering what happened, but remembering the pattern of what tends to happen next. That is what lets a skilled person recognize a situation before they can explain why.

And some memories seem to be stored most strongly as state-dependent packages. A smell, posture, song, location, or body state can suddenly restore an entire scene because the original memory was encoded across many channels at once.

So instead of:

memory = picture archive

a better model is:

memory = distributed reconstruction across sensory, motor, emotional, conceptual, bodily, spatial, and predictive systems.

That also explains why a memory can survive even when the “picture” is gone. You may not see the event clearly anymore, but you can still remember its rhythm, bodily posture, emotional temperature, sequence of movements, meaning, or what you were about to do next.

In that sense, every sense can have a memory, and every memory can be multisensory.